WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.733
Gymnasium.

2
00:00:02.033 --> 00:00:06.863
It is the standard contract between an environment and a learning algorithm.

3
00:00:06.863 --> 00:00:08.833
Any RL library can train on it.

4
00:00:10.033 --> 00:00:14.023
Gymnasium is the maintained fork of OpenAI Gym.

5
00:00:14.023 --> 00:00:16.400
Its idea: a shared contract.

6
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:18.283
Reset starts an episode.

7
00:00:18.283 --> 00:00:19.733
Step takes one action.

8
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:27.433
It also declares its observation space and action space up front, so libraries can build matching networks automatically.

9
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:29.633
Here is the loop.

10
00:00:29.933 --> 00:00:32.300
Reset hands over the first observation.

11
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:34.200
The agent chooses an action.

12
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:40.033
Step applies it, and returns a new observation, a reward, and whether the episode ended.

13
00:00:41.233 --> 00:00:45.300
Step returns two different kinds of ending, and they must not be confused.

14
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:50.233
Terminated means the problem itself finished, like a crash or reaching the goal.

15
00:00:50.533 --> 00:00:53.147
Truncated means we merely ran out of time.

16
00:00:53.147 --> 00:00:55.200
The state still has future value.

17
00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:00.500
So after termination the bootstrap value is zero, but after truncation it is not.

18
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.733
Mixing them up silently corrupts value estimates.

19
00:01:04.933 --> 00:01:06.833
Here is a tiny rover on a grid.

20
00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:08.573
First, the two spaces.

21
00:01:08.573 --> 00:01:12.500
Positions are observations, and four directions are actions.

22
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.167
Reset puts the rover at the start and returns its position.

23
00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:20.797
Step moves it, with a small penalty per move and a reward at the goal.

24
00:01:20.797 --> 00:01:23.767
The last two flags are terminated and truncated.

25
00:01:24.067 --> 00:01:26.500
And now PPO trains on it, unchanged.

26
00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:33.367
You already have a simulator of satellite battery dynamics, driven by historical telemetry.

27
00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:35.936
Wrap it in the four-method class.

28
00:01:35.936 --> 00:01:38.893
Observations are charge, rate and sunlight.

29
00:01:38.893 --> 00:01:41.300
Actions are heater on, off, or eco.

30
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:43.844
Now every algorithm can train on it.

31
00:01:43.844 --> 00:01:46.400
The wrapper is the real engineering work.

32
00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:48.405
Let's recap.

33
00:01:48.405 --> 00:01:52.567
Gymnasium is the contract between environments and algorithms.

34
00:01:52.867 --> 00:01:56.167
Two functions, reset and step, and two declared spaces.

35
00:01:56.467 --> 00:02:00.233
Terminated and truncated are different endings, so keep them apart.

36
00:02:00.533 --> 00:02:04.347
And wrapping your own problem is how you make it RL-ready.

37
00:02:04.347 --> 00:02:05.333
Next up: RLlib.
